# Linux runner for the multiplayer browser suite WITH a real, SIMULATING sim peer.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. `Dockerfile.harness` runs the browsers but nothing that needs a cell to be
# SIMULATED. Since `canSimulate` became `p.system === true`, only a sim peer may hold cell
# authority, and `s40-npc`, `s42-crowded-cell` and `s51-npc-combat` assert that NPCs actually move
# and fight — which `server/dist/testpeer.mjs` cannot do, because it answers the wire without
# running OpenMW. Those three are skipped behind OMW_SIM_PEER=1 until something can.
#
# WHY IT IS BUILT THIS WAY ROUND, which took two wrong turns to establish:
#
#   1. Add the peer to the harness image (Debian bookworm). Dead: the peer binary is linked
#      against Ubuntu 24.04's glibc 2.39 and bookworm has 2.36.
#   2. Rebase on Debian trixie (glibc 2.41, and a real chromium .deb — both constraints met) and
#      carry the peer's Ubuntu .so closure into a quarantined /opt/peerlib. Gets impressively
#      far — `openmw --version` runs, zero missing libraries — and then SEGFAULTS booting the
#      game (signal 11 at 0xac). Mixing an Ubuntu-built engine with Debian's glibc and mesa is
#      an ABI hazard, not a packaging detail. Do not retry this.
#
# So: keep the peer in the environment it was BUILT for and where it is already known to work
# (`STATUS.md` measured it there), and bring the browser to it instead. Ubuntu has no chromium
# .deb — it is snap-only, `apt-cache policy chromium` is empty — but Google publish a real
# google-chrome-stable .deb, which is the ordinary way CI gets a browser on Ubuntu. The peer
# image already ships node 22 and python 3.12, which is everything else the harness needs.
FROM openmw-simpeer:local
USER root

RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends wget gnupg ca-certificates \
 && wget -qO /tmp/chrome.deb https://dl.google.com/linux/direct/google-chrome-stable_current_amd64.deb \
 && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends /tmp/chrome.deb \
 && rm -f /tmp/chrome.deb && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# CHROME_BIN is what mp-harness.mjs launches; --no-sandbox is added whenever it is set, which is
# required in a container.
#
# SMOKE_GL=angle-swiftshader, NOT the raw swiftshader Dockerfile.harness uses, and that
# difference is why this image could not run a single scenario. mp-harness.mjs says it in its
# own comment: raw SwiftShader GL does not implement every entry point the engine probes, the
# engine resolves GL dynamically, and a missing one comes back null. The symptom is not an
# error, it is a WEDGE -- measured here, the browser sat at 0.0% CPU for 18 minutes with a
# crashpad process beside it, while the native sim peer ran perfectly at 21.5% and 491 MB. The
# half everyone expects to be fragile was fine; the browser was the problem.
#
# Raw swiftshader survives in Dockerfile.harness only because that image ships Debian's older
# chromium. This one carries google-chrome 152, where it does not. angle-swiftshader runs ANGLE
# -- the translator the engine targets in a real browser -- over SwiftShader's Vulkan, which is
# what mp-harness.mjs already recommends for a GPU-less Linux box.
ENV CHROME_BIN=/usr/bin/google-chrome \
    SMOKE_GL=angle-swiftshader \
    JOIN_TIMEOUT_MS=900000 \
    LOADING_CLEAR_MS=240000 \
    OMW_SIM_PEER_BIN=/usr/local/bin/openmw

WORKDIR /repo
